n.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e834c31-c35d-57df-a0ce-02370148728a" :question/math-academy-id "q-82089" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** **If** $2+3<4,$ **then** $1+5<8.$\n- **II.** **If** $1+5<8,$ **then** $2+3<4.$\n- **III.** **If** $1+5<4,$ **then** $2+3<8.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "An implication $A⇒B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement I is true. The antecedent $2+3<4$ is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The antecedent $1+5<8$ is true, and the consequent $2+3<4$ is false. So the promise is broken, and the statement is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Again, the antecedent $1+5<4$ is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "281873c8-e173-5181-8281-96b84d636b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Chinese Remainder Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbbe17e2-2245-5b19-baca-5456adac09aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471765} {:answer/id #uuid "08a66943-735d-5f31-9404-bb573f0d4a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbbe17e2-2245-5b19-baca-5456adac09aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471765} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb55755-746d-562f-9818-8bd362ec525c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472388} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33afde02-6c00-5acc-b627-671a3dd3be13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471767} {:answer/id #uuid "30552bbd-ad0a-50b5-ab48-2ca1fc8d73e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30552bbd-ad0a-50b5-ab48-2ca1fc8d73e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471768} :answer-field/id #uuid "0554ea35-bbf0-5e21-bd1a-586d4cd5d745" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472389} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e90c9bb9-0a65-5aa4-92c8-378c3f722004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471769} {:answer/id #uuid "5231270d-8208-54ce-9a8e-aedfc4af4fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e90c9bb9-0a65-5aa4-92c8-378c3f722004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471769} :answer-field/id #uuid "82ef0c81-c876-58a1-8875-d6aaea32054e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b00b20e-2b69-5a9e-a153-226cf102bd50" :question/math-academy-id "q-83978" :question/problem "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $48?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system I since $6$ and $8$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,8)=2>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system II. The numbers $3,$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 16=48.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system III since $4$ and $12$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c290820-a094-51ac-84b5-47702a109966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218647177