ef 17592186470684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83d36793-7a5a-5a98-bc17-ba820d516cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470683} :answer-field/id #uuid "76818222-e330-53b7-9af4-f5e80254a647" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccdaee72-7ba3-5cab-ba35-f5476892e5d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-103319" :question/problem "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $¬D?$\n- **I.** $¬D∨(C∧¬D)$\n- **II.** $¬D∨(¬D∧C)$\n- **III.** $(¬D∧C)∧¬D$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\n¬D∨(¬D∧C)≡¬D.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $¬D.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬D∨(C∧¬D) & ≡¬D∨(¬D∧C) \\\\  & ≡¬D.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $¬D.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬D∨(¬D∧C) & ≡¬D.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $¬D.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4ea352-2a82-5ff5-8c08-b7f9a09929e6" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Disjunction Absorbs Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75955220-3663-523d-9f9e-7f463108c24e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470685} {:answer/id #uuid "733ecdec-5955-5666-9935-d16529c69334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759